lesson:
```
std::string grade(int score)
{
    if (score >= 80) return "A";
    else if (score >= 65) return "B";
    else if (score >= 50) return "C";
    else return "F";
}
```

The conditions are checked top to bottom and the first true one wins, so order matters.

Combine conditions with `&&` (and), `||` (or) and `!` (not):

```
bool canFire = ammo > 0 && !reloading;
bool alarm = level < 10 || level > 95;
```

`switch` picks between fixed values of an `int` or `char`:

```
switch (key) {
    case 'w': y -= 1; break;
    case 's': y += 1; break;
    default: break;
}
```

Forgetting `break` makes it "fall through" into the next case. And the classic C++ bug: `if (health = 0)` sets health to 0. The compiler warns about it, which is one reason to read warnings.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main()
{
    int ammo = 3;
    bool reloading = false;
    if (ammo > 0 && !reloading) std::cout << "fire\n";
    else std::cout << "wait\n";
    char key = 'w';
    switch (key) { case 'w': std::cout << "up\n"; break; default: std::cout << "?\n"; }
    return 0;
}

